``peat pillage`` now supports disk images on macOS. DMG, sparse image and bundle, ISO, and raw images are attached read-only with ``hdiutil`` (no root needed), other formats (VMDK, VHD, VHDX, qcow2) are converted with ``qemu-img`` first, and every volume macOS can read is searched, including every volume in an APFS container. Encrypted DMG images and encrypted APFS volumes are unlocked with passwords from the new ``--password-file`` option, or prompted for in a terminal, and BitLocker and LUKS volumes are skipped with a message pointing to Linux. Pillaging a live macOS system (``/``) skips the Data volume (which is already searched through firmlinks), mounted volumes, and macOS system directories, explains how to give the terminal Full Disk Access when macOS blocks a folder, and skips iCloud files that haven't been downloaded.
